def X(id,title,lane,status,pr,start,ev,main,alts,src,img=None,end=None,changed=None,dateNote=None):
  d=dict(id=id,title=title,lane=lane,status=status,priority=pr,start=start,end=end,ev=ev,main=main,alts=alts,src=src,img=img)
  if changed:d['changed']=changed
  if dateNote:d['dateNote']=dateNote
  return d
E=[]
# --- part 1: science that changed the picture
E+=[
X('penicillin-1928','Penicillin: the mould on a forgotten dish',H,'confirmed',1,1928,['documentary','laboratory records','scientific publication'],
 "In September 1928 the Scottish bacteriologist Alexander Fleming, working at St Mary's Hospital in London, noticed that a mould contaminating one of his Staphylococcus culture plates had killed the bacteria around it. He identified the mould as a Penicillium, called the active substance 'penicillin' and published his findings in 1929, but he could not purify it in useful amounts. In 1940 a team at the University of Oxford led by Howard Florey and Ernst Chain purified penicillin and showed that it cured infections in mice and then people. Mass production during the Second World War made it the first widely used antibiotic. Fleming, Florey and Chain shared the 1945 Nobel Prize in Physiology or Medicine.",
 [('Luck or preparation?','The story is often told as a pure accident. Later reconstructions suggest that an unusual run of cool and then warm weather in London, while Fleming was on holiday, helped the mould and bacteria grow in just the right order. Others stress that Fleming had studied antibacterial substances for years and recognised what he saw.'),
  ('Who gets the credit','Press coverage in the 1940s focused on Fleming, and some historians argue that the Oxford team, including biochemist Norman Heatley, did much of the work that turned a curiosity into a medicine. Mouldy food used in folk remedies in several cultures is sometimes cited as a forerunner; the evidence for this is anecdotal.'),
  ('A warning in 1945','In his Nobel lecture Fleming warned that misuse of penicillin could lead to resistant bacteria. Antibiotic resistance is now considered a major global health problem.')],
 ['wiki:Penicillin','wiki:History_of_penicillin','wiki:Alexander_Fleming'],img='Alexander Fleming',
 changed='1928: mould noticed. 1929: published. 1940-41: purified and tested at Oxford. 1945: Nobel Prize. Since then: growing antibiotic resistance.'),
X('smallpox-eradication','From milkmaids to eradication: the defeat of smallpox',H,'confirmed',1,1796,['documentary','epidemiological','medical records'],
 "Smallpox killed hundreds of millions of people over the centuries. Long before vaccines, people in China, India, the Ottoman Empire and parts of Africa practised 'variolation', deliberately infecting a person with material from a mild case. In 1796 the English doctor Edward Jenner tested a rural belief that milkmaids who caught cowpox did not get smallpox: he inoculated a boy, James Phipps, with cowpox and then showed he resisted smallpox. The word 'vaccine' comes from the Latin vacca, cow. In 1967 the World Health Organization launched an intensified eradication campaign built on surveillance and ring vaccination. The last natural case was in Somalia in 1977, and in 1980 the WHO declared smallpox eradicated, the only human disease wiped out so far.",
 [('Folk knowledge first','Jenner was not the first to try cowpox; a farmer, Benjamin Jesty, is recorded as having inoculated his family in 1774. In 1721 Onesimus, an enslaved African man in Boston, described variolation as practised in West Africa to Cotton Mather, who promoted it during an epidemic. Historians cite these cases as examples of traditional knowledge that formal medicine later adopted.'),
  ('Should the last samples be destroyed?','Stocks of the virus are still kept in two high-security laboratories, in the United States and Russia. Whether to destroy them has been debated at the WHO for decades.')],
 ['wiki:Smallpox_vaccine','wiki:Smallpox','wiki:Edward_Jenner'],img='Edward Jenner',end=1980,dateNote='1796 Jenner experiment; eradication declared 1980',
 changed='1796: Jenner\'s cowpox experiment. 1967: WHO intensified campaign. 1977: last natural case. 1980: eradication declared.'),
X('mendel-peas','Mendel\'s peas: the forgotten laws of heredity',H,'confirmed',2,1866,['experimental','scientific publication'],
 "Gregor Mendel, an Augustinian friar at St Thomas's Abbey in Brno (now in the Czech Republic), bred thousands of pea plants between 1856 and 1863. Tracking seven traits, such as seed colour and plant height, he found that traits are passed on as separate units in predictable ratios. A trait could vanish in one generation and return in the next, about one in four times. He called traits 'dominant' and 'recessive' and published in 1866. His work drew little attention in his lifetime. In 1900 three botanists, Hugo de Vries, Carl Correns and Erich von Tschermak, independently reached similar results and rediscovered his paper. His 'factors' are now called genes, and his work is seen as the foundation of genetics.",
 [('Too good to be true?','In 1936 the statistician Ronald Fisher argued that Mendel\'s ratios were closer to the expected values than chance would allow, suggesting that the data had been tidied. Others have defended Mendel, pointing to how he may have chosen and counted plants. The debate has never been fully settled.'),
  ('Why was he ignored?','Explanations include his use of mathematics, unusual in biology at the time, publication in a local journal, and the fact that he later turned to running his abbey. Some historians argue he was less ignored than the legend says.')],
 ['wiki:Gregor_Mendel','wiki:Mendelian_inheritance','wiki:Experiments_on_Plant_Hybridization'],img='Gregor Mendel',
 changed='1866: published. 1900: rediscovered by de Vries, Correns and Tschermak. 1936: Fisher questions the data. 2025: the last of Mendel\'s seven pea genes reported identified.'),
X('expanding-universe-1929','The universe is expanding: Lemaître, Hubble and the receding galaxies',H,'confirmed',1,1929,['astronomical observation','theoretical'],
 "In the 1910s Vesto Slipher measured that most spiral 'nebulae' show redshifted light, meaning they are moving away from us. In 1922 Alexander Friedmann showed that Einstein's general relativity allowed an expanding universe. In 1927 the Belgian priest and physicist Georges Lemaître proposed that the universe is expanding and that recession speed rises with distance. In 1929 Edwin Hubble, using distances to galaxies measured at Mount Wilson Observatory, published the relation between distance and velocity. This expansion is the basis of the Big Bang model. In 2018 the International Astronomical Union recommended calling it the Hubble–Lemaître law to recognise Lemaître's earlier work.",
 [('Tired light and steady state','In 1929 Fritz Zwicky suggested that light might lose energy over vast distances ("tired light") rather than galaxies receding; this idea is rejected by most astronomers today. From 1948 Fred Hoyle, Hermann Bondi and Thomas Gold proposed a "steady state" universe that expands forever without a beginning. The discovery of the cosmic microwave background in 1965 weighed heavily against it.'),
  ('Einstein\'s "biggest blunder"','Einstein is often quoted as calling his cosmological constant his "biggest blunder" after expansion was found. The quote comes second-hand from the physicist George Gamow, and historians question whether Einstein said it.'),
  ('The Hubble tension','Different methods give different values for the expansion rate today. Whether this points to measurement problems or to new physics is an open question.')],
 ["wiki:Hubble's_law",'doi:10.1073/pnas.15.3.168','wiki:Georges_Lemaître'],img='Edwin Hubble',
 changed='1912-1917: Slipher\'s redshifts. 1922: Friedmann. 1927: Lemaître. 1929: Hubble. 1965: CMB found. 2018: renamed Hubble–Lemaître law.'),
X('pulsars-lgm-1967','"Little Green Men": the pulsar that looked like a signal',H,'confirmed',2,1967,['radio astronomy','scientific publication'],
 "In August 1967 Jocelyn Bell, a PhD student at the University of Cambridge, noticed a small, odd trace in the paper chart records of a new radio telescope she had helped build. It turned out to be a source pulsing every 1.337 seconds with clock-like precision. Because no natural source this regular was known, the team half-jokingly labelled it LGM-1, for 'Little Green Men', and briefly considered whether it could be artificial. Finding more sources in different parts of the sky made that unlikely. The 1968 paper in Nature, led by Antony Hewish, reported the first pulsar, now known to be a rapidly spinning neutron star sweeping beams of radio waves past Earth like a lighthouse.",
 [('A signal from aliens?','The alien idea was dropped within months, once several pulsars were found and a natural explanation fitted. The episode is often told to show how scientists handle a signal that looks artificial; it is cited alongside the 1977 Wow! signal, which has never been repeated or explained.'),
  ('The Nobel controversy','The 1974 Nobel Prize in Physics went to Hewish and Martin Ryle but not to Bell (later Bell Burnell). Some astronomers, such as Fred Hoyle, criticised the decision. Bell Burnell has said she was not upset at the time. In 2018 she received a Breakthrough Prize and gave the money to fund graduate students from under-represented groups.')],
 ['wiki:Pulsar','doi:10.1038/217709a0','wiki:Jocelyn_Bell_Burnell'],img='Jocelyn Bell Burnell'),
X('vera-rubin-dark-matter','Vera Rubin and the galaxies spinning too fast: the case for dark matter',H,'disputed',2,1970,['astronomical observation','spectroscopy'],
 "In the 1970s the American astronomer Vera Rubin and her colleague Kent Ford measured how fast stars and gas orbit the centres of spiral galaxies, starting with Andromeda. If most of a galaxy's mass were in its bright centre, outer stars should orbit more slowly, as outer planets do around the Sun. Instead their speeds stayed roughly flat far out. This implied large amounts of unseen mass. Together with Fritz Zwicky's 1933 observation that galaxies in the Coma Cluster moved too fast to be held together by their visible matter, and later radio measurements, Rubin's work made 'dark matter' part of mainstream cosmology. Most estimates now say it makes up about 85% of the matter in the universe.",
 [('What is it?','Decades of experiments have not detected a dark matter particle directly. Candidates include weakly interacting particles, axions and primordial black holes. Until something is found, its nature is unknown.'),
  ('Or is gravity different?','A minority of physicists favour modifying the laws of gravity instead, for example Mordehai Milgrom\'s Modified Newtonian Dynamics (MOND), proposed in 1983. Supporters say it predicts galaxy rotation well; critics say it struggles with galaxy clusters and the cosmic microwave background.'),
  ('Overlooked','Rubin never received a Nobel Prize, which some scientists consider an oversight. The Vera C. Rubin Observatory in Chile is named after her.')],
 ['wiki:Vera_Rubin','doi:10.1086/150317','wiki:Dark_matter','wiki:Galaxy_rotation_curve'],img='File:Vera Rubin measuring spectra.jpg',
 changed='1933: Zwicky\'s "dunkle Materie". 1970s: Rubin and Ford\'s rotation curves. 1983: MOND proposed. Today: still not directly detected.'),
X('first-exoplanet-1995','51 Pegasi b: the first planet found around a Sun-like star',H,'confirmed',2,1995,['astronomical observation','spectroscopy'],
 "In October 1995 the Swiss astronomers Michel Mayor and Didier Queloz announced a planet orbiting the star 51 Pegasi, about 50 light-years away. Using precise spectra from the Haute-Provence Observatory in France, they detected the star wobbling as an unseen companion tugged on it. The planet turned out to be a surprise: at least half Jupiter's mass but orbiting closer to its star than Mercury is to the Sun, completing a 'year' in about four days. Such 'hot Jupiters' were not predicted by theories of planet formation. The discovery opened a flood of finds; more than 5,000 exoplanets are now confirmed. Mayor and Queloz shared the 2019 Nobel Prize in Physics.",
 [('Earlier claims','In 1992 Aleksander Wolszczan and Dale Frail found planets orbiting a pulsar, a dead star, which are accepted as the first confirmed exoplanets. A 1988 claim of a planet around Gamma Cephei was treated with caution at the time and confirmed in 2003. A 1991 pulsar planet claim was retracted.'),
  ('Are we alone?','Exoplanet discoveries have fuelled debate on how common Earth-like planets and life might be. Many astronomers think rocky planets in habitable zones are common; whether life exists on any of them is unknown.')],
 ['wiki:51_Pegasi_b','doi:10.1038/378355a0','wiki:Exoplanet'],img='51 Pegasi b',
 changed='1992: pulsar planets. 1995: 51 Pegasi b. 2003: Gamma Cephei b confirmed. 2019: Nobel Prize. 2022: NASA count passes 5,000.'),
X('human-genome-2003','Reading the human genome',H,'confirmed',2,1990,['genetic','scientific publication'],
 "The Human Genome Project was an international effort, begun in 1990, to read the sequence of the roughly three billion DNA 'letters' in human chromosomes. A rival private company, Celera Genomics, led by Craig Venter, raced to sequence it too. A draft was announced jointly in June 2000 and published in February 2001, and the project was declared complete in April 2003, although about 8% of the genome, mostly highly repetitive regions, was still missing. That gap was closed in 2022 by the Telomere-to-Telomere consortium. One of the biggest surprises was that humans have only about 20,000 protein-coding genes, far fewer than the 50,000 to 100,000 many had expected.",
 [('How much is "junk"?','Only about 1-2% of the genome codes for proteins. In 2012 the ENCODE project claimed that about 80% of the genome had some biochemical function; many geneticists disputed that definition, and the share that is truly functional is still debated.'),
  ('Ancient DNA inside us','Genome sequencing later showed that most people outside Africa carry a small percentage of Neanderthal DNA, and some populations carry Denisovan DNA, changing how human origins are understood.')],
 ['wiki:Human_Genome_Project','doi:10.1038/35057062','doi:10.1126/science.abj6987'],img='Human Genome Project',end=2003,dateNote='1990-2003; gapless sequence 2022',
 changed='1990: project begins. 2000: draft announced. 2001: published. 2003: declared complete. 2022: first gapless sequence.'),
X('indian-ocean-tsunami-2004','The 2004 Indian Ocean tsunami and the island that remembered',H,'confirmed',1,2004,['seismological','documentary','oral tradition'],
 "On 26 December 2004 a magnitude 9.1-9.3 earthquake struck off the west coast of Sumatra, Indonesia, one of the largest ever recorded. It set off tsunamis that crossed the Indian Ocean, striking Indonesia, Sri Lanka, India, Thailand and as far as Somalia and South Africa. Around 230,000 people died in 14 countries, making it one of the deadliest natural disasters in recorded history. There was no tsunami warning system in the Indian Ocean at the time; one was set up afterwards. On Simeulue, an island very close to the epicentre, few people died, which researchers credit to a local oral tradition, smong, passed down since a 1907 tsunami: when the ground shakes and the sea draws back, run to high ground.",
 [('Memory as a warning system','Simeulue, along with reports that Moken sea nomads in Thailand and Myanmar fled to high ground, is often cited as evidence that traditional stories can preserve useful knowledge of rare disasters. Researchers note that such traditions work best when the last event is recent enough to be remembered.'),
  ('Animals that sensed it?','Many reports said animals fled before the waves arrived. Scientists say animals may have reacted to the earthquake shaking or to the sound of the approaching waves; there is no clear evidence of a special "sixth sense".')],
 ['wiki:2004_Indian_Ocean_earthquake_and_tsunami','wiki:Simeulue','doi:10.1126/science.1112250'],img='2004 Indian Ocean earthquake and tsunami'),
X('tohoku-2011','Tōhoku 2011: the tsunami, Fukushima and the stones that warned',H,'confirmed',1,2011,['seismological','documentary','geological'],
 "On 11 March 2011 a magnitude 9.0-9.1 earthquake off north-east Japan, the most powerful ever recorded in the country, generated a tsunami that reached heights of up to about 40 metres. Around 18,000 people died or are missing. At the Fukushima Daiichi nuclear power plant the tsunami knocked out cooling systems, and three reactors suffered meltdowns, the worst nuclear accident since Chernobyl and rated at the maximum level 7. Geologists had found sand layers showing that a similar great tsunami, the Jōgan tsunami of 869, had swept far inland on the same coast, and old stone markers along the coast warned later generations not to build below certain points.",
 [('Warnings in stone and sand','Some villages that followed old tsunami stones, such as Aneyoshi, where a stone reads "Do not build your homes below this point", were spared. Researchers had warned before 2011 that the Jōgan evidence implied a larger risk than planners assumed; how far that warning was taken into account is debated.'),
  ('Radiation and health','Most experts conclude that radiation from Fukushima caused few or no measurable health effects, while the evacuation itself caused many deaths among elderly people. Some groups dispute these assessments. The release of treated water into the sea from 2023 also remains controversial.')],
 ['wiki:2011_Tōhoku_earthquake_and_tsunami','wiki:Fukushima_nuclear_accident','wiki:869_Sanriku_earthquake'],img='Fukushima nuclear accident'),
X('halifax-explosion-1917','The Halifax Explosion: the largest human-made blast before the atomic bomb',H,'confirmed',3,1917,['documentary','photographic','legal records'],
 "On the morning of 6 December 1917 the French cargo ship Mont-Blanc, loaded with wartime explosives, collided with the Norwegian ship Imo in the harbour of Halifax, Nova Scotia. Fire broke out on Mont-Blanc and about 20 minutes later it exploded with the force of roughly 2.9 kilotons of TNT. At least 1,782 people were killed and about 9,000 injured. The Richmond district was flattened, and a tsunami from the blast destroyed a Mi'kmaq community at Tufts Cove. Train dispatcher Vince Coleman stayed at his post to telegraph a warning to an incoming passenger train and was killed. It remained the largest human-made explosion until atomic weapons were tested in 1945.",
 [('Who was to blame?','The first inquiry blamed Mont-Blanc. A later appeal, decided ultimately by the Privy Council in London, found both ships at fault. Writers still debate the roles of the pilots, harbour rules and wartime secrecy about the cargo.')],
 ['wiki:Halifax_Explosion','wiki:Vince_Coleman'],img='File:Halifax Explosion blast cloud restored.jpg'),
]
# --- part 2: ancient teachers, texts and tombs
E+=[
X('hyksos','The Hyksos: foreign kings of Egypt, and an echo in the Exodus story?',H,'confirmed',2,-1650,['archaeological','textual','isotopic'],
 "The Hyksos, from an Egyptian phrase meaning 'rulers of foreign lands', were the kings of Egypt's Fifteenth Dynasty, about 1650 to 1550 BCE. They ruled Lower and Middle Egypt from Avaris in the Nile Delta, where excavations at Tell el-Dab'a, led by Manfred Bietak, revealed a city with strong Levantine (Canaanite) features in its houses, burials and goods. They were driven out by the Theban king Ahmose I, who founded the New Kingdom. Later Egyptian texts portrayed Hyksos rule as a disaster, but they also seem to have brought new technologies to Egypt, such as the horse-drawn chariot and the composite bow, though how many were introduced by them is debated.",
 [('Invasion or slow takeover?','The priest-historian Manetho, writing in the 3rd century BCE, described a violent invasion. Many Egyptologists now think Levantine settlers grew in the Delta over generations before taking power. A 2020 study of strontium isotopes in teeth from Avaris found many people born outside Egypt over a long period, which its authors argued fits gradual migration better than a sudden conquest.'),
  ('The Exodus link','The Jewish historian Josephus, quoting Manetho in the 1st century CE, linked the expulsion of the Hyksos to the Israelites leaving Egypt. Some modern writers have revived versions of this idea; most scholars see no direct evidence connecting the two and note that the dates do not clearly match.')],
 ['wiki:Hyksos','doi:10.1371/journal.pone.0235414','wiki:Avaris'],img='Hyksos',end=-1550,dateNote='Fifteenth Dynasty, c. 1650-1550 BCE'),
X('enheduanna','Enheduanna: the first author known by name?',H,'disputed',2,-2300,['textual','archaeological'],
 "Enheduanna was the high priestess of the moon god Nanna at Ur, in southern Mesopotamia, around 2300 BCE. She was the daughter of Sargon of Akkad, who united Mesopotamia into the first empire, and was probably appointed to bind the religion of his Akkadian court to the older Sumerian traditions. A limestone disc found at Ur in the 1920s by Leonard Woolley's excavations shows her taking part in a ritual and names her. Several Sumerian hymns, including the 'Exaltation of Inanna', speak in her voice or name her as their composer. On this basis she is often called the earliest named author in history, about 1,500 years before Homer.",
 [('Did she write them?','The surviving copies of the hymns were made centuries after her lifetime, in the Old Babylonian period. Some Assyriologists argue that the texts were attributed to her later, or reworked so much that her authorship cannot be proven; others accept that at least some of the core is hers.'),
  ('A modern icon','Enheduanna has become a symbol in feminist history and poetry. Historians note that her prominence also reflects her royal birth and the political role her father gave her.')],
 ['wiki:Enheduanna','wiki:Sargon_of_Akkad'],img='Enheduanna'),
X('confucius','Confucius: the teacher who became a state philosophy',H,'confirmed',1,-551,['textual','historical tradition'],
 "Kong Qiu, known in Chinese as Kongzi or Kong Fuzi ('Master Kong') and in the West as Confucius, was a teacher and minor official from the state of Lu, in what is now Shandong, traditionally dated 551 to 479 BCE. He lived during the Spring and Autumn period, when the Zhou kingdom had split into rival states. He taught that good government depends on rulers of good character, on ritual, and on respect within the family and society. His sayings were collected by his followers in the Analects. Under the Han dynasty, from the 2nd century BCE, Confucian teachings became the basis of state education, and the imperial examinations on Confucian texts lasted until 1905.",
 [('How much is really his?','The Analects were compiled after his death, probably over generations. Scholars debate which sayings go back to Confucius himself and which reflect the views of later disciples.'),
  ('Sage, god or reactionary?','Confucius was later honoured in temples and given imperial titles, and some regard Confucianism as a religion while others call it a philosophy. During the 1973-74 "Criticise Lin, Criticise Confucius" campaign in China he was attacked as a defender of the old order; today he is again officially honoured.')],
 ['wiki:Confucius','wiki:Analects','url:https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/confucius/|Stanford Encyclopedia of Philosophy: Confucius|encyclopedia'],img='Confucius',end=-479),
X('laozi-daodejing','Laozi and the Daodejing: the sage who rode west',H,'disputed',2,-500,['textual','archaeological (manuscripts)','legend'],
 "Laozi ('Old Master') is the traditional author of the Daodejing, the founding text of Daoism, which teaches harmony with the Dao (the 'Way') through simplicity and effortless action (wu wei). The historian Sima Qian, writing around 100 BCE, gave a short biography: Laozi was an archivist of the Zhou court, older than Confucius, who met him. Disillusioned, he rode west on an ox; at the frontier pass the gatekeeper Yinxi asked him to write down his teachings, and he left the Daodejing before disappearing. Sima Qian himself admitted uncertainty, offering other possible identities. The oldest known copies of the text are bamboo slips from a tomb at Guodian, buried around 300 BCE and found in 1993.",
 [('A real person?','Many scholars think the Daodejing is a compilation of sayings that grew over time, and that "Laozi" may be a legendary or composite figure. Others accept that a historical teacher lies behind the tradition.'),
  ('Laozi becomes a god','By the Han dynasty Laozi was worshipped as a deity, later as Taishang Laojun, one of the highest gods of religious Daoism. A later text, the Huahu jing, claimed that he travelled to India and became the Buddha; Buddhists rejected it, and Chinese emperors ordered it destroyed more than once.')],
 ['wiki:Laozi','wiki:Tao_Te_Ching','wiki:Guodian_Chu_Slips'],img='File:Zhang Lu-Laozi Riding an Ox.jpg',dateNote='Traditionally 6th century BCE; Guodian manuscripts c. 300 BCE'),
X('the-buddha','The Buddha: Siddhartha Gautama and the birthplace dug up',H,'confirmed',1,-480,['textual','archaeological','inscriptions'],
 "Buddhism traces its origins to Siddhartha Gautama, a prince of the Shakya clan born at Lumbini in what is now Nepal. According to tradition he left his palace after seeing old age, sickness and death, sought liberation as a wandering ascetic, and attained awakening under a tree at Bodh Gaya, becoming the Buddha ('awakened one'). He taught for decades in the Ganges plain and died at Kushinagar, aged about 80. Dates vary: older accounts give about 563-483 BCE; many scholars now prefer about 480-400 BCE. Around 249 BCE the emperor Ashoka erected a pillar at Lumbini with an inscription saying 'here the Buddha was born'.",
 [('Dating the Buddha','Different Buddhist traditions and modern scholars disagree on his dates by more than a century. In 2013 archaeologists led by Robin Coningham reported a timber structure beneath the Maya Devi Temple at Lumbini, which they dated to about the 6th century BCE and suggested was an early shrine; others question how securely it can be linked to the Buddha.'),
  ('History and legend','Accounts of his life, including miracles at his birth, were written down centuries after his death. Scholars generally accept that he was a historical person while treating many details as later tradition.')],
 ['wiki:The_Buddha','wiki:Lumbini','wiki:Lumbini_pillar_inscription'],img='The Buddha',dateNote='Traditionally c. 563-483 BCE; many scholars c. 480-400 BCE'),
X('pythagoras','Pythagoras: mathematician, mystic and miracle-worker',H,'disputed',2,-570,['textual (later sources)','historical tradition'],
 "Pythagoras of Samos, born about 570 BCE, settled in Croton in southern Italy, where he founded a community of followers bound by rules about diet, silence and secrecy. The Pythagoreans taught that the soul is reborn in new bodies and that numbers underlie the order of the world, including musical harmony, an idea later expanded into the 'music of the spheres'. No writings by Pythagoras survive, and almost everything known about him comes from authors writing centuries later. Ancient biographers credited him with miracles: a golden thigh, being seen in two cities at once, and speaking to animals.",
 [('Did he discover "his" theorem?','The relationship now called Pythagoras\'s theorem was known in Babylonia over a thousand years earlier, as the clay tablet Plimpton 322 (c. 1800 BCE) shows, and appears in early Indian texts. Whether Pythagoras himself proved it is uncertain; ancient sources credit discoveries of his school to the master.'),
  ('Secret brotherhood','His community was attacked and dispersed in a political uprising. Later esoteric movements, from Neoplatonists to some modern secret societies, claimed descent from Pythagorean wisdom; historians see these as inspired by, not continuous with, the original school.')],
 ['wiki:Pythagoras','wiki:Plimpton_322','url:https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/pythagoras/|Stanford Encyclopedia of Philosophy: Pythagoras|encyclopedia'],img='Pythagoras',dateNote='c. 570 - c. 495 BCE'),
X('aristarchus-heliocentrism','Aristarchus: the Sun-centred universe, 1,800 years before Copernicus',H,'confirmed',2,-270,['textual'],
 "Aristarchus of Samos (about 310 to 230 BCE) was a Greek astronomer who proposed that the Earth goes around the Sun and spins on its own axis. His book on the idea is lost; we know of it because Archimedes described it in The Sand Reckoner. His one surviving work, On the Sizes and Distances of the Sun and Moon, used geometry to argue that the Sun is far larger than the Earth, which may be why he put it at the centre. Most Greek astronomers rejected the idea, partly because they could not detect the shift in star positions (parallax) that a moving Earth implied, and the Earth-centred system later set out by Ptolemy dominated for centuries.",
 [('Did Copernicus know?','Copernicus mentioned Aristarchus in a passage of his manuscript that he later crossed out. Historians debate how much the ancient idea influenced him.'),
  ('Why was he ignored?','Some ancient writers say a philosopher, Cleanthes, thought Aristarchus should be charged with impiety. Historians generally think the idea failed mainly for scientific reasons of the time, since stellar parallax was not measured until 1838.')],
 ['wiki:Aristarchus_of_Samos','wiki:Heliocentrism','wiki:The_Sand_Reckoner'],img='Aristarchus of Samos',dateNote='c. 310 - c. 230 BCE'),
X('emerald-tablet','The Emerald Tablet: "as above, so below"',M,'myth-legend',3,800,['textual','legend'],
 "The Emerald Tablet is a short, cryptic text attributed to Hermes Trismegistus ('thrice-great Hermes'), a legendary sage combining the Greek god Hermes and the Egyptian god Thoth. The earliest known versions are in Arabic, in alchemical works of the 8th to 10th centuries CE such as the Secret of Creation, often with a story that the text was found on an emerald tablet in Hermes' tomb. Latin translations reached Europe from the 12th century, and the text became a cornerstone of alchemy, read as instructions for making the philosopher's stone. Its best-known line is usually paraphrased as 'as above, so below'. Isaac Newton made his own translation.",
 [('Ancient Egyptian wisdom?','Renaissance scholars believed the Hermetic writings preserved wisdom older than Moses. In 1614 Isaac Casaubon showed that the related Greek Hermetic texts dated from the early Christian era. The Emerald Tablet itself is not known before the Arabic sources.'),
  ('Esoteric readings','Occultists and New Age writers treat the tablet as a key to hidden laws linking the cosmos and the self, and some link Hermes to lost civilisations such as Atlantis. These readings are speculative and not supported by historical evidence.')],
 ['wiki:Emerald_Tablet','wiki:Hermes_Trismegistus'],img='Emerald Tablet',dateNote='Earliest Arabic versions 8th-10th century CE; claimed to be far older'),
X('zhang-qian','Zhang Qian: the envoy who opened the Silk Road',H,'confirmed',2,-138,['textual'],
 "In about 138 BCE the Han emperor Wu sent an official, Zhang Qian, west to find the Yuezhi people and form an alliance against the Xiongnu nomads. Zhang was captured by the Xiongnu and held for about ten years, married and had a son, then escaped and reached Central Asia, including Ferghana and Bactria. The Yuezhi had settled down and refused the alliance. Captured again on the way home, he finally returned around 125 BCE, with only one of his original companions. His reports of rich cities, Persian-ruled lands and the 'heavenly horses' of Ferghana led the Han to expand west and open regular trade along what was later called the Silk Road. His travels are recorded by Sima Qian.",
 [('Did he "create" the Silk Road?','Goods and ideas already moved across Central Asia before him, as finds of Chinese silk far to the west suggest. Historians see his mission as the start of official Chinese contact rather than the first exchange.'),
  ('Exploration as legend','Later Chinese tradition credited Zhang Qian with bringing back grapes, alfalfa and other plants; scholars note that some of these attributions are doubtful.')],
 ['wiki:Zhang_Qian','wiki:Silk_Road'],img='Zhang Qian',end=-125),
X('lindow-man','Lindow Man: a bog body, a ritual death and an unexpected confession',H,'confirmed',2,-2,['archaeological','radiocarbon','forensic'],
 "In August 1984 peat cutters at Lindow Moss near Wilmslow, Cheshire, found the preserved upper body of a man. Lindow Man was a healthy man in his mid-20s with trimmed nails and beard. He seems to have been struck on the head, garrotted with a cord and had his throat cut before being placed face-down in the bog, sometime between about 2 BCE and 119 CE. The find caused a media sensation and is now in the British Museum. A year earlier, workers in the same moss had found a woman's skull. A local man, believing it was his wife's, confessed to killing her decades earlier; radiocarbon dating later showed the skull was from the Roman era, but he was still convicted on his confession.",
 [('A druid sacrifice?','A 1989 book by Anne Ross and Don Robins argued that Lindow Man was a high-status druid sacrificed in a "triple death". Many archaeologists consider this too speculative, and some suggest some injuries happened after death or that he was executed or murdered.'),
  ('Bog bodies across Europe','Lindow Man is one of hundreds of bog bodies found in northern Europe, many of whom died violently. Scholars often link them to offerings or executions.')],
 ['wiki:Lindow_Man','wiki:Bog_body'],img='File:Lindow Man 1.jpg',dateNote='c. 2 BCE - 119 CE'),
X('maeshowe','Maeshowe: the solstice tomb and the Viking graffiti',H,'confirmed',3,-2800,['archaeological','astronomical alignment','runic inscriptions'],
 "Maeshowe is a Neolithic chambered cairn on Mainland Orkney, Scotland, built around 2800 BCE. A long, low passage leads to a central chamber of finely fitted stone slabs. Around the winter solstice, the setting Sun shines along the passage and lights the back wall of the chamber. In the 12th century Norse men broke into the mound and carved more than 30 runic inscriptions, one of the largest collections in the world. Some boast that treasure was carried away, others mention women, and one shows a carved creature often called the Maeshowe dragon. It is part of the Heart of Neolithic Orkney World Heritage Site.",
 [('Built for the Sun?','The solstice alignment is widely accepted. Some researchers argue it was deliberate to mark the turning of the year, linked with beliefs about death and rebirth; others caution that ancient intentions cannot be known for certain.'),
  ('Treasure and the hogboon','The Norse runes claim treasure was taken, but no contents of the tomb survive and the claim cannot be checked. Orkney folklore told of a mound-dweller, the hogboon, living in such mounds.')],
 ['wiki:Maeshowe','url:https://www.historicenvironment.scot/visit-a-place/places/maeshowe-chambered-cairn/|Historic Environment Scotland: Maeshowe Chambered Cairn|heritage'],img='Maeshowe'),
]
# --- part 3: hidden worlds, apparitions, 'impossible' animals and southern Africa
E+=[
X('agartha','Agartha: the kingdom inside the Earth',M,'myth-legend',2,1873,['literary','esoteric','speculative'],
 "Agartha is a legendary underground kingdom, usually placed beneath Central Asia and ruled by a hidden 'King of the World' who secretly guides events on the surface. The idea is modern. It first appeared in 1873 in Les fils de Dieu by the French writer Louis Jacolliot, who claimed to draw on ancient Indian manuscripts. It was developed by the occultist Saint-Yves d'Alveydre in the 1880s, popularised by Ferdynand Ossendowski's 1922 travel book Beasts, Men and Gods, and discussed by René Guénon in The King of the World (1927). From the 1960s, writers such as Raymond Bernard merged it with Hollow Earth ideas, with entrances at the poles.",
 [('Hidden masters','Esoteric and New Age movements have described Agartha as home to an advanced race or spiritual masters, sometimes linked to Shambhala, Atlantis or UFOs. There is no physical evidence for such a realm, and these claims are speculative.'),
  ('Literary origins','Historians trace the legend to 19th-century European fascination with India and Tibet. Some note that Ossendowski\'s account closely follows Saint-Yves\'s earlier book, which suggests borrowing.'),
  ('The real underground','Geophysics shows that Earth\'s interior is solid and molten rock and metal, not hollow; seismic waves from earthquakes are used to map its layers.')],
 ['wiki:Agartha','wiki:Hollow_Earth'],img='Agartha'),
X('bell-witch','The Bell Witch: America\'s most famous haunting',M,'myth-legend',2,1817,['folklore','later written accounts'],
 "According to Tennessee legend, from 1817 to 1821 the family of farmer John Bell in Robertson County was tormented by an invisible entity. It knocked on walls, pulled bedclothes, slapped and pinched the children, especially daughter Betsy, and eventually spoke, sang and argued with visitors. It claimed to be the spirit of a neighbour, 'Kate Batts', among other identities. When John Bell died in 1820, the spirit was said to have boasted that it had poisoned him. The best-known account, Martin Ingram's Authenticated History of the Bell Witch, appeared in 1894, more than 70 years later. A cave on the former Bell farm is now a tourist site.",
 [('Andrew Jackson\'s visit','A famous episode says General Andrew Jackson came to investigate and his party fled. It appears only in a letter printed in Ingram\'s book, and historians have found no contemporary record of it.'),
  ('Hoax, poltergeist or folklore?','Sceptics point out that no account written during the haunting itself is known, and one 1880s newspaper story about the witch was linked to a known hoaxer. Believers treat the case as a classic poltergeist. Folklorists study it as a legend that grew over generations.')],
 ['wiki:Bell_Witch','wiki:Poltergeist'],img='Bell Witch',end=1821),
X('persian-princess-2000','The "Persian Princess": a mummy that turned out to be a murder case',H,'debunked',2,2000,['forensic','radiocarbon','documentary'],
 "In October 2000 Pakistani police recovered a mummy that was reportedly being offered on the black antiquities market for 600 million Pakistani rupees (about US$11 million at the time). It lay in a carved coffin with a gold crown and a breastplate inscribed in cuneiform, which identified her as a daughter of the Persian king Xerxes, about 2,500 years ago. Iran and Pakistan both claimed her. Asma Ibrahim, curator of the National Museum of Pakistan, soon noticed features that raised doubts. The philologist Rüdiger Schmitt found many grammatical errors in the Old Persian text, parts of which had been copied from the Behistun inscription, radiocarbon dating put the coffin at only about 250 years old, and examination suggested the woman had died only a few years earlier. Police treated the case as a possible murder; the woman's identity has never been established.",
 [('A forgery ring','Investigators suspected that the body was obtained, and possibly the woman killed, to make a saleable fake antiquity. Several people were arrested, but the full story has not come out.'),
  ('How it fooled people','The case is often cited as a warning about how convincing forgeries can be, and how the antiquities market can encourage crime.')],
 ['wiki:Persian_Princess'],img=None),
X('guadalupe-1531','Our Lady of Guadalupe: the vision at Tepeyac',H,'disputed',2,1531,['religious tradition','textual','material (the tilma)'],
 "According to Catholic tradition, in December 1531 the Virgin Mary appeared several times to Juan Diego, an Indigenous convert, on the hill of Tepeyac near Mexico City, ten years after the Spanish conquest of the Aztec capital. Asked for a sign by the bishop, Juan Diego gathered roses in winter in his cloak (tilma); when he opened it, an image of the Virgin was revealed. The tilma hangs today in the Basilica of Our Lady of Guadalupe, one of the most visited Catholic shrines in the world. The fullest early account, the Nican Mopohua in Nahuatl, was published in 1649. Juan Diego was canonised in 2002, and Guadalupe became a symbol of Mexican identity.",
 [('Historical doubts','Some historians note the lack of surviving documents from the 1530s about the apparitions. In the 1990s the basilica\'s own former abbot, Guillermo Schulenburg, questioned whether Juan Diego existed, causing controversy. Supporters cite Indigenous traditions and later testimonies.'),
  ('Tonantzin and the hill','A 16th-century friar, Bernardino de Sahagún, wrote that Tepeyac had been a place of worship for a mother goddess called Tonantzin. Some scholars see the Guadalupe devotion as blending Indigenous and Catholic beliefs; others reject this as overstated.'),
  ('Studies of the image','Some art experts have identified paint and later retouching on the tilma. Believers have claimed that tiny human figures can be seen reflected in the Virgin\'s eyes; sceptics describe this as pareidolia.')],
 ['wiki:Our_Lady_of_Guadalupe','wiki:Juan_Diego','wiki:Nican_Mopohua'],img='Our Lady of Guadalupe'),
X('lourdes-1858','Lourdes: Bernadette\'s visions and the healing spring',H,'disputed',2,1858,['testimony','religious tradition','medical records'],
 "Between February and July 1858, Bernadette Soubirous, a 14-year-old miller's daughter in Lourdes in south-western France, reported 18 visions of a young lady in a grotto called Massabielle. Crowds followed her; at the lady's direction she dug in the ground and a spring appeared. Asked her name, the lady reportedly said, in the local dialect, 'I am the Immaculate Conception'. The Catholic Church approved the apparitions in 1862, and Lourdes became one of the world's largest pilgrimage sites. A medical bureau founded in 1883 examines claimed cures; only about 70 have been formally recognised as miracles by the Church. Bernadette became a nun and was canonised in 1933.",
 [('Miracle or natural recovery?','Sceptics argue that with millions of visitors, rare spontaneous recoveries are expected, and that the small number of recognised cures is itself telling. Supporters point to the strict medical checks before any cure is recognised.'),
  ('A child as witness','Bernadette was questioned repeatedly by officials who suspected fraud or illness and kept to her account. The case is often compared with other child visionaries, such as the children of Fátima in 1917.')],
 ['wiki:Our_Lady_of_Lourdes','wiki:Bernadette_Soubirous','wiki:Lourdes_apparitions'],img='Our Lady of Lourdes',end=1858),
X('platypus-1799','The platypus: the animal scientists thought was a hoax',H,'confirmed',2,1799,['zoological specimens','scientific publication'],
 "When the first platypus skin reached Britain from New South Wales in the late 1790s, it looked like a joke: a duck's bill on a furry, beaver-like body with webbed feet. The naturalist George Shaw, who described it in 1799, wrote that it was hard not to suspect deception, and some scientists thought it might be a fake stitched together, like the 'mermaids' sold to collectors. Further specimens proved it was real. Its biology kept surprising: it detects prey with electroreceptors in its bill, the male has a venomous spur, and it lays eggs. Egg-laying was finally confirmed in 1884, when William Caldwell sent a famous telegram from Australia: 'Monotremes oviparous, ovum meroblastic'.",
 [('Scissor marks','Shaw is said to have used scissors to check the specimen for stitches. Some at the time suspected that it had been made by sailors who sewed a duck\'s beak onto another animal.'),
  ('Lessons in doubt','The platypus is often cited both as a reason for scientific caution and as a warning that some "impossible" reports turn out to be true. Indigenous Australians knew the animal well long before Europeans debated it.')],
 ['wiki:Platypus','wiki:Monotreme'],img='Platypus',
 changed='1799: described, suspected fake. Early 1800s: more specimens confirm it. 1884: egg-laying confirmed.'),
X('komodo-dragon-1910','The Komodo dragon: a rumoured "land crocodile" turns out to be real',H,'confirmed',2,1910,['zoological specimens','scientific publication','local tradition'],
 "Pearl divers and fishermen in the Lesser Sunda Islands of Indonesia had long told of huge lizards. Around 1910 the Dutch colonial officer J. K. H. van Steyn van Hensbroek visited Komodo after hearing reports of a 'land crocodile', and obtained a specimen. In 1912 Peter Ouwens, director of the zoological museum at Buitenzorg (Bogor) in Java, described it scientifically as Varanus komodoensis. It is the largest living lizard, up to about 3 metres long. The Komodo people also call the lizards sebae, 'twins', from a traditional belief that they share the same spiritual mother as the dragons. Komodo dragons live only on Komodo, Rinca, Flores and a few nearby islets, now largely within Komodo National Park.",
 [('A dragon legend?','Some writers suggest such animals could have inspired Chinese or other dragon legends; there is no direct evidence for this. The dragon\'s discovery is often cited by cryptozoologists as proof that large unknown animals can still be found, while zoologists note that it was well known locally all along.'),
  ('Island giants','Flores was also home to Homo floresiensis, the small human species found in 2003, and to dwarf elephants. Scientists study the island as an example of how size changes on islands.')],
 ['wiki:Komodo_dragon','wiki:Komodo_National_Park'],img='Komodo dragon',dateNote='First European specimen c. 1910; described 1912'),
X('wollemi-pine-1994','The Wollemi pine: a "living fossil" found in a hidden canyon',H,'confirmed',2,1994,['botanical','fossil comparison','genetic'],
 "In September 1994 David Noble, a New South Wales national parks officer, was exploring a remote gorge in the Wollemi National Park, about 150 km north-west of Sydney, when he noticed unusual trees. They turned out to be a previously unknown conifer of the ancient Araucariaceae family, named Wollemia nobilis. Similar trees are known from fossils dating back tens of millions of years, so it was hailed as a 'living fossil'. Fewer than 100 adult trees grow in the wild, and their location is kept secret. In the 2019-20 bushfires, firefighters set up irrigation and dropped fire retardant to save the grove. Cloned trees are now sold and planted worldwide to protect the species.",
 [('How "living" a fossil?','Scientists caution that "living fossil" can mislead: the species has continued to evolve, though its family has a long fossil record. Genetic studies show the wild trees have very low genetic diversity.'),
  ('A pattern of rediscovery','The Wollemi pine is often grouped with the coelacanth and other "Lazarus" species that were thought long extinct, showing that remote places can still hide surprises.')],
 ['wiki:Wollemia'],img='Wollemia'),
X('moshoeshoe-thaba-bosiu','Moshoeshoe and Thaba Bosiu: the mountain that "grew at night"',H,'confirmed',2,1824,['oral tradition','documentary','archaeological'],
 "Moshoeshoe I (about 1786-1870) was the founder of the Basotho nation and the first king of Lesotho. In July 1824, during the upheavals known as the Mfecane or Difaqane, he moved his people to a flat-topped sandstone mountain he named Thaba Bosiu, 'mountain at night', because they arrived there at night. To scare off enemies, he spread word that the mountain grew larger at night. It was never taken by force. Moshoeshoe gave cattle and protection to refugees, welcomed French missionaries in 1833, and fought wars with the Boer Orange Free State. In 1868 he obtained British protection, and the territory became Basutoland, later independent Lesotho.",
 [('Strategy and legend','Historians see the "growing mountain" as an example of deliberate psychological warfare, while stories about it remain part of Basotho tradition. Moshoeshoe\'s reputation for diplomacy, including feeding defeated enemies, is central to national memory.'),
  ('Contested histories','The Mfecane itself is debated: older accounts blamed the Zulu kingdom under Shaka for widespread chaos, while some historians since the 1980s argue that colonial slave raiding and drought also played major roles.')],
 ['wiki:Moshoeshoe_I','wiki:Thaba_Bosiu'],img='Moshoeshoe I',end=1870),
X('kaggen-mantis','ǀKaggen: the San trickster who becomes a mantis',M,'myth-legend',2,1870,['oral tradition','ethnographic records','rock art'],
 "ǀKaggen, often called 'Mantis' in English, is a creator and trickster figure of the ǀXam San of southern Africa. He can change shape, usually becoming a praying mantis but also an eland, a snake or a louse. He gets into trouble and plays tricks, and the eland is his favourite creation: in one story his sons kill his hidden eland, and blood from it, churned in a pot, spatters onto the ground and turns into snakes. Much of what is known comes from the Bleek and Lloyd archive: in the 1870s the linguist Wilhelm Bleek and Lucy Lloyd recorded thousands of pages of stories in Cape Town from ǀXam men such as ǁKabbo, some of them prisoners. The archive is listed in UNESCO's Memory of the World register.",
 [('Reading the rock art','Since the 1970s many researchers, notably David Lewis-Williams, have interpreted San rock paintings of eland and part-human figures as linked to trance and to beliefs like those in the ǀXam records. Others warn against applying 19th-century ǀXam beliefs to art made by different groups over thousands of years.'),
  ('A culture recorded on the edge of loss','The ǀXam language died out soon after it was recorded, so the archive is a rare surviving voice. Some descendants and scholars stress that it was collected under colonial conditions.')],
 ['wiki:ǀKaggen','wiki:Wilhelm_Bleek'],img='Mantis',dateNote='Recorded 1870s; tradition much older'),
X('molasses-flood-1919','The Great Molasses Flood: a 35 mph wave of syrup',H,'confirmed',3,1919,['documentary','photographic','legal records'],
 "On 15 January 1919 a huge storage tank holding about 8.7 million litres of molasses burst in the North End of Boston, Massachusetts. A wave of molasses several metres high rushed through the streets at an estimated 56 km/h, knocking buildings off their foundations and trapping people and horses. Twenty-one people died and about 150 were injured. A long court case found that the tank, owned by a subsidiary of United States Industrial Alcohol, had been poorly built and never properly tested; it had leaked so badly that it was painted brown to hide the leaks. Residents said the area smelled of molasses on hot days for decades.",
 [('Why so deadly?','In 2016 a team of scientists and students at Harvard University studied the disaster and suggested that the cold January air made the molasses thicken quickly, trapping victims and hampering rescuers.'),
  ('The anarchist defence','The company claimed the tank had been blown up by anarchists. The court rejected this and blamed the tank\'s construction; the case helped push for stricter engineering checks and building codes.')],
 ['wiki:Great_Molasses_Flood'],img='Great Molasses Flood'),
]
